  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Abdomen Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Always wait at least 30 to 60 minutes after eating so the stomach is not actively digesting a full meal. Use one or two fingertips at roughly 90 degrees to the skin, allowing 10 to 15 seconds to deepen slowly into each point. For most midline points, 30 to 60 seconds per side works for a quick session and one to two minutes for a deeper feel. Avoid pressing over surgical scars, and stop if any spot feels tender or sharp rather than deeply full.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can stomach points help with bloating and digestion?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Points such as CV12 and ST25 are commonly used in self-care for bloating and general digestive discomfort. Gentle pressure, combined with waiting 30 to 60 minutes after meals, can help the belly relax and may make digestive symptoms feel less intrusive. Acupressure is not a proven cure for digestive disorders, so persistent pain, unexplained weight loss, or blood in the stool should always be checked by a clinician rather than managed with pressure alone.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is abdominal acupressure safe during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">During pregnancy, avoid direct pressure on the abdomen, and follow the general cautions used across this site, including avoiding the points LI4, SP6, BL60, and GB21. Lower-midline points such as CV6 and CV4 are classically treated with extra care during pregnancy. Always ask a qualified practitioner, midwife, or doctor before starting any acupressure routine while pregnant. If pressing anywhere feels wrong or uncomfortable, stop and talk to a professional.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For abdominal points, 30 to 60 seconds per point is a good starting range, and one to two minutes works when you want a deeper, more settling feel. Press roughly 90 degrees to the skin, deepen slowly over 10 to 15 seconds, and work both sides for paired points. Aim for a comfortable deqi response—deep, dull, slightly heavy or full, never sharp—and breathe evenly while holding. Wait 30 to 60 minutes after a meal before you begin.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to Body Atlas</a>
  </div>
</article>
</BaseLayout>